  "query_preview": "..\nToday through tomorrow afternoon: An upper-level low off the \nWashington coast is moving southeastward, and will bring a few rain \nbands through the forecast area as the day goes on. Snow levels are \n5000 to 6000 feet, and little to no snow accumulations are expected. \nPrecipitation totals will overall be low across the Inland \nNorthwest, with 0.05-0.10 inches expected. Higher rainfall totals \nwill be confined to far SE Washington and the central ID Panhandle, \nwhere rainfall totals could reach 0.3-0.35 inches. Though the low \nwill move out of the area by late tonight, residual moisture will \nkeep showers across the mountains, WA/ID Palouse, and ID Panhandle. \nFor the Spokane to Wenatchee area and southward, there will be a \nbreak in precipitation Tuesday morning through afternoon. \n\nTuesday evening through Thursday: On Tuesday evening, another \nstronger low pressure system will move southward down into the \nforecast area. This will be a colder system, with snow levels \nlowering to 2000 to 3000 feet. Snow will start late Tuesday night in \nthe Cascades, then will start in the northern mountains and ID \nPanhandle early Wednesday morning. Stevens and Washington Passes \nhave the highest probabilities of receiving 6 inches or more of snow \nover 48 hours, with a 35-40% chance. Other passes, including \nSherman, Lookout, and Blewett Passes, have a 15% chance or less of 4 \ninches. This will create winter driving conditions at Stevens and \nWashington Pass, and a Winter Weather Advisory has been issued for \nthese passes from Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday afternoon. \nElsewhere, expect light precipitation. Temperatures will be cooler \nafter the low moves through, with highs in the 40s and low 50s and \nlows in the 30s. \n\nFriday through Monday: The low will remain just off the Washington \ncoast for Friday and Saturday, weakening each day. As it weakens, \nchances for precipitation decrease. Main chances for showers are in \nthe mountains due to orographic lift. Long-range models show snow \nlevels dropping to 2000 feet over north central Washington, but \ngiven the decrease in precipitation chances, snow accumulations are \nnot expected at this elevation. Any lingering showers will be light. \nSunday into monday, the low will shift southward and a ridge will \nbuild into the area, prompting  warm-up into Monday. Ensemble \nclusters show 80% support for this pattern, favoring a warming trend \ninto Monday. /AS",
